window.__finlexDocLoaded(10073,{"id":10073,"articles":[{"id":1000224,"no":"Overview","heading":"Official Overview & Scope (IFRS Foundation)","text":"IFRS 8 requires an entity whose debt or equity securities are publicly traded to disclose information to enable users of its financial statements to evaluate the nature and financial effects of the different business activities in which it engages and the different economic environments in which it operates. It specifies how an entity should report information about its operating segments in annual financial statements and in interim financial reports. It also sets out requirements for related disclosures about products and services, geographical areas and major customers.","seq":0},{"id":1000225,"no":"IFRS 8.5–28","heading":"Management Approach, CODM & Reportable Segment Thresholds","text":"• Chief Operating Decision Maker — CODM (IFRS 8.5–10): identify operating segments based on internal reports regularly reviewed by the CODM to allocate resources and assess performance.\n• Quantitative thresholds (IFRS 8.13–15): report separately any operating segment (or aggregated similar segments under 8.12) meeting ≥10% of combined revenue, ≥10% of absolute profit/loss, or ≥10% of combined assets; total external revenue of reportable segments must reach at least 75% of entity revenue.\n• Entity-wide disclosures (IFRS 8.31–34): products/services, geographical areas, and major customers representing ≥10% of total revenue (converged with China CAS 35).","seq":1},{"id":1000226,"no":"History","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In April 2001 the International Accounting Standards Board (Board) adopted IAS 14 Segment Reporting, which had originally been issued by the International Accounting Standards Committee in August 1997. IAS 14 Segment Reporting replaced IAS 14 Reporting Financial Information by Segment, issued in August 1981.\n\nIn November 2006 the Board issued IFRS 8 Operating Segments to replace IAS 14. IAS 1 Presentation of Financial Statements (as revised in 2007) amended the terminology used throughout the Standards, including IFRS 8.\n\nOther Standards have made minor consequential amendments to IFRS 8. They include IAS 19 Employee Benefits (issued June 2011), Annual Improvements to IFRSs 2010–2012 Cycle (issued December 2013) and Amendments to References to the Conceptual Framework in IFRS Standards (issued March 2018), Disclosure of Accounting Policies (issued February 2021) and IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements (issued April 2024).","seq":2},{"id":1000227,"no":"Support","heading":"Related Projects & Implementation Support","text":"[Related completed projects]\n• Aggregation of Operating Segments (Amendments to IFRS 8)\n• Disclosure of Revenues and Expenses for Reportable Segments (IFRS 8)\n• Equity Method in Separate Financial Statements (Amendments to IAS 27)\n• Improvements to IFRS 8 Operating Segments\n• Interim Financial Reporting and Segment Information for Total Assets and Liabilities (Amendments to IAS 34)\n• Post-implementation Review of IFRS 8 Operating Segments\n• Reconciliation of the Total of the Reportable Segments' Assets to the Entity's Assets (Amendments to IFRS 8)\n• Segment Reporting\n\n[Implementation support]\n• IFRS 8 supporting material","seq":3},{"id":1000228,"no":"References","heading":"Official Text Link & Public Practical Guidance","text":"Official IFRS Foundation Page (English): https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/ifrs-8-operating-segments/\nAccess Note: Free access to the unaccompanied text of IFRS Accounting Standards on ifrs.org requires a free registered IFRS.org account; Basis for Conclusions and Illustrative Examples require an IFRS Digital Subscription.\nPublic Companion Overview: KPMG Insights into IFRS — An Overview (Sept 2026): https://assets.kpmg.com/content/dam/kpmgsites/xx/pdf/ifrg/2026/isg-2026-insights-an-overview.pdf","seq":4}]});
